it is a hidden horror of an already  harrowing period at some vincent's  hospital in westchester  the pandemic drove some people away  quiet corridors that filled instead with  tales of isolation  and grief specialists at the sharp end  of the crisis have begun calling it the  silent  pandemic as those with mental health and  addiction issues  become increasingly disconnected and  unable to cope  28 year old eric bender used to come  here  five days a week for treatment things  were  looking up eric  was a drug user for 10 years and he had  been silver  for the last year he was looking for a  job  we felt he had turned the corner and  then  kovit came and he isolated in his  apartment  all by himself he couldn't see the  counselor he valued so much in person  anymore  it was just calls after self-isolating  he started to spiral he had his birthday  on  april 9th and on  may 5th he passed away from a drug  overdose  so it was a dramatic shift in his life  covered 19. it was dramatic and he is  collateral damage  if the pandemic hadn't come eric would  be alive  what do you want people to know i love  derek  and he worked real hard but he didn't  make it  and there's nothing to be ashamed of  in fact we're rather proud  that he did work so hard  he died after taking cocaine laced with  fentanyl a synthetic opioid  his mother firmly believes he didn't  intend to die  but like so many his physical and  emotional  tolerance was tested a lot of our  patients are experiencing  grief and loss unexpectedly that they  didn't anticipate experiencing coming  into march  loss of a job loss of a family member  loss of  sense of purpose and identity we've just  seen  more severe illness and a spike of it  and then we've also not been able to see  patients in person  group interaction is considered a key  part of getting better but that is still  not an option  when covid came all of a sudden people  who were used to  reconnecting with peers and people and  and uh socializing again were all of a  sudden forced into isolation  and that was the hardest part so many  people got  sad they got depressed their best friend  all of a sudden was a drug of choice  we saw a lot of people uh returning to  use struggling with relapse  and ultimately overdoses just you know  skyrocketing  from my own experience i know three  people personally  who died within one week  in may there was a 48 rise in suspected  overdoses in america compared to the  previous year  and here has been a sharp increase in  new people needing mental health support  their doors are open they want people to  come and they're finding new ways to  treat them  but there is no quick fix and months  maybe  years of struggle ahead cordelia lynch  sky news harrison new york
 

